        "original_id": "67d60693-e99d-5ce1-8715-ca8512e29751",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Ken Kesey, Tom Wolfe\n- Products: Further (Furthur) bus, The Electric Kool-Aid Acid Test, Magic Trip\n- Groups: Merry Band of Pranksters\n- Concepts: New Journalism, counterculture\n\nFacts:\n- Further, also known as Furthur, is a 1939 International Harvester school bus.\n- In 1964, author Ken Kesey purchased Further to carry his Merry Band of Pranksters across the United States while filming their counterculture adventures.\n- In 1968, Tom Wolfe published The Electric Kool-Aid Acid Test as a nonfiction book.\n- The bus Further featured prominently in Tom Wolfe's 1968 book The Electric Kool-Aid Acid Test.\n- The Electric Kool-Aid Acid Test is remembered as an early and arguably the most popular example of New Journalism.\n- Because of the trip's chaos and editing difficulties, footage from Ken Kesey and the Merry Band of Pranksters' journey was not released as a movie until the 2011 documentary Magic Trip.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
